---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import Main from "@/layouts/Main.astro";
import ProjectPreviewTitle from "@/components/ProjectPreviewTitle.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/config";
---

<Layout title={`Projects | ${SITE.title}`}>
  <Header />
  <Main
    pageTitle="Projects"
    pageDesc="Projects I've built — from secure backend systems and deployed full-stack products to team collaborations, testing, and cloud coursework."
  >
    <ul class="space-y-6">
      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">CaseFlow Ops</h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Individual backend project · Jul 2026 – present · In progress
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          A legal matter workflow platform for a small Australian law firm,
          developed with synthetic data. It draws on my Master of Laws
          background: I am building the backend as a series of secure vertical
          slices, focusing on explicit access rules, transactional workflows,
          testable API contracts, and reproducible database evolution. The React
          frontend is still a scaffold and is not yet connected to the API.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          Java 21 · Spring Boot · Spring Security · JJWT · Spring Data JPA ·
          PostgreSQL 16 · Flyway · JUnit · MockMvc · Docker Compose · GitHub
          Actions
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">Implemented</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Implemented BCrypt credential verification and stateless JWT
              authentication, reloading the current user from PostgreSQL on each
              protected request so deleted accounts and role changes take effect
              immediately.
            </li>
            <li>
              Combined Partner, Lawyer, Paralegal, and Client roles with
              case-assignment and client-ownership checks, enforced at the
              service boundary rather than only on HTTP routes.
            </li>
            <li>
              Built transactional Draft case creation with automatic Lead Lawyer
              assignment, plus client creation with case-insensitive duplicate
              protection backed by PostgreSQL constraints and consistent 409
              Conflict responses.
            </li>
            <li>
              Added explicit response models to keep persistence relationships
              and internal legal notes out of API responses.
            </li>
            <li>
              Wrote 37 unit, web-security (MockMvc), and PostgreSQL integration
              tests covering authentication, denied access, role changes,
              account deletion, and database constraints.
            </li>
            <li>
              Recorded key trade-offs in architecture decision records, with
              append-only Flyway migrations and GitHub Actions quality gates on
              every pull request.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">In progress</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Paginated, role-restricted client directory API with stable
              ordering.
            </li>
            <li>
              Client portal linking, case status transitions, and React
              authentication with protected routes.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a
            href="https://github.com/zxl3011/caseflow-ops"
            class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            GitHub →
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/zxl3011/caseflow-ops/tree/main/docs/decisions"
            class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            Design Decisions →
          </a>
          <span class="text-sm text-foreground/40">No live demo yet</span>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <ProjectPreviewTitle
          headingLevel="h2"
          href="https://stitchflow.xinluzhang.workers.dev/"
          imageAlt="StitchFlow project dashboard showing search, navigation, and a sample crochet workflow"
          imageSrc="/stitchflow-preview.jpg"
          title="StitchFlow"
        />
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Individual full-stack product · Jul 2026 – present · OpenAI Build Week
          2026
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          A terminology-aware workspace for knitters and crocheters who work
          across English, Japanese, and Chinese patterns. I evolved StitchFlow
          from a local-first Build Week prototype into an account-based product
          that securely synchronises structured projects, progress, images, and
          measurement profiles across devices, while keeping sign-in optional.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          TypeScript · React · Vite · Supabase Auth · PostgreSQL · Row-Level
          Security · Cloud Storage · Cloudflare Workers · GitHub Actions ·
          Tailwind CSS
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">Highlights</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Designed a typed Project, Section, Part, Row, Round, and Step
              domain model so real patterns are not flattened into a generic
              checklist.
            </li>
            <li>
              Built text-first pattern entry with stitch-count checks that
              calculate each row from its instruction and flag mismatches,
              numbering gaps, and mixed US/UK terminology.
            </li>
            <li>
              Recognised mixed English, Chinese, Japanese, and chart shorthand
              through a source-recorded glossary that never silently guesses
              unknown terms.
            </li>
            <li>
              Added Google and email authentication, password reset, owner-only
              row-level security, private user-scoped image storage, and
              security headers.
            </li>
            <li>
              Built an explicit local-to-cloud migration flow so existing users
              can import device projects without silent overwrites.
            </li>
            <li>
              Implemented an in-browser photo colour preview using k-means
              clustering in OKLab inside a Web Worker, so photos are never
              uploaded.
            </li>
            <li>
              Delivered English and Simplified Chinese interfaces, accessibility
              controls, print-to-PDF templates, and continuous deployment to
              Cloudflare Workers.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">In progress</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Rule-based PDF pattern import that runs entirely in the browser,
              scored against a local evaluation set.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a
            href="https://stitchflow.xinluzhang.workers.dev/"
            class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            Live Demo →
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://youtu.be/x8B2VpBRKBw"
            class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            Video Demo →
          </a>
          <span class="text-sm text-foreground/40">Private repository</span>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">
          Surfboard Rental Management System
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Individual full-stack project · Jan 2026 – Jun 2026
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          A full-stack board rental platform supporting registration and login,
          availability search, booking creation, and rental record management. I
          owned the full lifecycle from data modelling and REST API design
          through to a live deployment.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          Java 21 · Spring Boot · Spring Data JPA · React · TiDB/MySQL · Docker
          · Render · Vercel
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">Highlights</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Applied JPQL date-overlap conditions to exclude already-booked
              boards and prevent double bookings.
            </li>
            <li>
              Added request validation, unique email constraints, and
              centralised exception handling for consistent error responses.
            </li>
            <li>
              Used TiDB, a MySQL-compatible distributed SQL database, in the
              live environment, with connections configured through environment
              variables.
            </li>
            <li>
              Deployed a Dockerised backend to Render and the React frontend to
              Vercel, adding a warm-up request and loading state to handle
              free-tier cold starts.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a
            href="https://surfboard-rental-management.vercel.app"
            class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            Live Demo →
          </a>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/zxl3011/surfboard-rental-management"
            class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            GitHub →
          </a>
          <span class="text-sm text-foreground/40">Demo accounts in README</span
          >
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Workforce Time Management System</h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Team backend project · Mar 2026 – Jun 2026 · 11-person multinational
          Agile team · Code not public
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          A workforce management backend built with Java Spring Boot and MySQL,
          supporting staff administration, salary records, standard working
          hours, overtime rules, penalty configuration, and role-based access
          control data. I worked as a backend developer alongside a PM, business
          analysts, and frontend and backend colleagues.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          Java · Spring Boot · MySQL · REST APIs · Azure DevOps · Postman
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">My Contributions</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Clarified requirements, business rules, and delivery priorities
              directly with the PM and BAs.
            </li>
            <li>
              Developed backend APIs and business validation for staff
              administration and standard working hours.
            </li>
            <li>
              Implemented salary history logic so new pay rates apply only to
              future records while preserving historical payroll data.
            </li>
            <li>
              Built and collaborated on configuration APIs for overtime and
              penalty settings, and on role data supporting access control.
            </li>
            <li>
              Tracked tasks and defects in Azure DevOps and validated normal,
              error, and boundary scenarios with Postman during frontend
              integration.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Fearless Timetable Optimiser</h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Four-person course project · Apr 2026 – Jun 2026 · High Distinction ·
          Code not public
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          An AI-assisted Java command-line timetable application, generated and
          refined through prompt engineering from a written specification, then
          rigorously tested to evaluate the risks of AI-generated code.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          Java 17 · JUnit 5 · Maven · Prompt engineering
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">My Contributions</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Implemented and tested the ClassManager, TimetableManager, and
              console interaction components.
            </li>
            <li>
              Designed and executed 60 JUnit 5 tests covering CSV import,
              combined search, duplicate data, invalid input, and exceptional
              boundaries.
            </li>
            <li>
              Found three logic defects: case-sensitive timetable-name
              uniqueness, inconsistent substring matching, and an incomplete
              equals/hashCode that let records silently replace each other.
            </li>
            <li>
              Helped consolidate the team’s 141 executed tests (136 passing)
              into defect evidence and post-fix regression recommendations.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">YouTube Trender</h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Two-person course project · 2025 S2 · Code not public
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          A Java Swing desktop application that reads YouTube Data API JSON
          files and supports video display, sorting, search, and trend-term
          analysis.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          Java · Swing · JSON parsing · JUnit 5 · SVN
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">My Contributions</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Implemented the video domain model and JSON parser, handling file
              access failures, malformed content, and missing fields through
              custom exceptions.
            </li>
            <li>
              Built an inverted trend-term index with HashMap, using HashSet to
              avoid double-counting terms within a video.
            </li>
            <li>
              Maintained 72 JUnit 5 tests with 100% class, 81% method, and 76%
              line coverage, alongside Javadoc and SVN history.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Indigenous Art Atlas</h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Team full-stack project · 2025 S2 · Code not public
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          An interactive web application for exploring Indigenous artworks on a
          map. The platform used Leaflet.js and OpenStreetMap on the frontend
          and a PHP/MySQL backend to manage users, artwork data, artist
          profiles, collections, and access-controlled views.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          PHP · MariaDB/MySQL · JavaScript · AJAX · Leaflet.js · Role-based
          access
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">My Contributions</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Implemented user registration, login, password hashing, sessions,
              and role-based navigation for public users, artists, and
              administrators.
            </li>
            <li>Built artist profile and artist application workflows.</li>
            <li>Developed user collection and favourite artwork features.</li>
            <li>Used prepared statements for safer database access.</li>
            <li>
              Integrated backend data with frontend pages through PHP rendering
              and AJAX interactions.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a
            href="https://indigenous-art-atlas.onrender.com"
            class="text-sm text-accent hover:underline"
          >
            Live Demo →
          </a>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">
          University Smart Parking Assistant
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Team course project · 2026 S1 · Code not public
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          A conversational assistant that helps students find parking on campus
          through natural-language questions.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          IBM watsonx Assistant · Node-RED · Firebase · Google Maps API
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">My Contributions</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Owned the watsonx Assistant natural-language layer, configuring
              parking actions and example phrases.
            </li>
            <li>
              Integrated watsonx API requests through Node-RED, extracting
              intent, confidence, and response objects to route requests.
            </li>
            <li>
              Normalised action IDs into service categories such as nearest car
              park, emptiest car park, and notification requests, and took part
              in multi-turn conversation testing.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">
          Cloud Data Pipeline and Data Quality
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Individual coursework · 2025 S1
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          Serverless data collection and data-quality analysis completed for
          Data Engineering and Cloud Computing coursework.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          Python · AWS Lambda · DynamoDB · EventBridge · S3 · Glue DataBrew ·
          QuickSight
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">Highlights</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Configured a Python Lambda function, triggered by EventBridge
              every five minutes, to call an external API and write JSON records
              to DynamoDB.
            </li>
            <li>
              Assessed data-quality risks such as multilingual text, reposts,
              spam, and emoji, and proposed extensions with Glue DataBrew,
              QuickSight, and S3.
            </li>
            <li>
              Profiled 200 healthcare records for missingness, consistency, and
              anomalies, and proposed an AWS data architecture.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="rounded-lg border border-border p-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">
          SDG Tabletop Game – Application Testing
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-0.5 text-xs text-foreground/50">
          Four-person course project · 2026 S1
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-foreground/80">
          A black-box test strategy for an online multiplayer tabletop game
          covering player and facilitator dashboards, trades, project execution,
          voting, and results.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-foreground/60">
          Black-box testing · Boundary-value analysis · Decision tables ·
          Negative testing
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">My Contributions</h3>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-foreground/80">
            <li>
              Designed and executed 20 test cases for the trade, project
              execution, and voting modules.
            </li>
            <li>
              Verified consistency of ownership, cash, remaining time, and
              impact metrics after normal and abnormal operations.
            </li>
            <li>
              Found four failure scenarios, including insufficient-funds trades,
              edits after time expiry, and inconsistent voting thresholds, and
              documented regression recommendations.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
